NAME
       tar - create, list and unpack archives (with gzip, bzip2 or xz)

SYNOPSIS
       tar -c [-z|-j|-J|-a] [-v] -f ARCHIVE FILE...
       tar -t [-v] -f ARCHIVE
       tar -x [-v] -f ARCHIVE [-C DIRECTORY] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       tar puts many files and directories into one archive file, keeping
       their names, owners, permissions and times, and unpacks them again.
       By itself tar does not compress; with -z, -j or -J it passes the
       archive through gzip, bzip2 or xz. The usual file name endings are
       .tar, .tar.gz (or .tgz), .tar.bz2 and .tar.xz.

       Every tar command needs exactly one main mode: -c create, -t list,
       -x extract (or -r append). -f names the archive file; put the file
       name right after f, which is why it comes last in a group of letters:
       tar -czf backup.tar.gz /etc, not tar -cfz.

       The dash may be left out of the first group: tar czf ... is the same as
       tar -czf ... .

       When creating, tar removes the leading / from absolute paths and says
       so ("Removing leading `/' from member names"), so that extracting
       never overwrites files in their original place by surprise: the
       archive of /etc unpacks into ./etc in the current directory.

       When extracting, tar recognises the compression by itself, so
       tar -xf works for .tar.gz, .tar.bz2 and .tar.xz files.

OPTIONS
   Modes
       -c, --create
              Create a new archive.

       -t, --list
              List the archive's contents.

       -x, --extract
              Unpack the archive (into the current directory, or into -C).

       -r, --append
              Add files to the end of an uncompressed archive.

   Other options
       -f, --file=ARCHIVE
              The archive file to create or read.

       -v, --verbose
              List the files as they are processed; with -t, in ls -l style.

       -z, --gzip
              Compress (or uncompress) with gzip.

       -j, --bzip2
              Compress with bzip2.

       -J, --xz
              Compress with xz.

       -a, --auto-compress
              Pick the compression from the archive's name.

       -C, --directory=DIR
              Change to DIR first: extract into DIR, or, when creating, take
              the following names relative to DIR.

       -p, --preserve-permissions
              Extract the exact permissions stored in the archive (the default
              for root).

       -P, --absolute-names
              Keep the leading / of file names.

       -k, --keep-old-files
              Do not overwrite existing files when extracting.

       --exclude=PATTERN
              Leave out files whose names match PATTERN.

       --selinux, --acls, --xattrs
              Also store and restore SELinux contexts, ACLs and extended
              attributes. Without them, extracted files get default contexts
              and lose their ACLs.

EXAMPLES
       Make a gzip-compressed backup of /etc:

           tar -czf /root/etc-backup.tar.gz /etc

       Make a bzip2-compressed archive of /var/log, keeping SELinux contexts:

           tar --selinux -cjf /root/logs.tar.bz2 /var/log

       List what is in an archive without unpacking it:

           tar -tvf /root/etc-backup.tar.gz

       Unpack an archive into /tmp/restore:

           mkdir -p /tmp/restore
           tar -xzf /root/etc-backup.tar.gz -C /tmp/restore

       Restore a single file from it:

           tar -xzf /root/etc-backup.tar.gz -C /tmp/restore etc/hosts

NOTES
       Check an archive's compression with the file command:
       file /root/etc-backup.tar.gz says "gzip compressed data".

SEE ALSO
       gzip(1), bzip2(1), xz(1), zip(1), file(1)
